00:00:23.299 --> 00:00:27.619
So with this one in particular is
how do I form a triacylglyceride?

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.490
Well it's pretty straight
forward how to do it.

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:35.764
So if I have a glycerol, which is a three
member alcohol, so we see three alcohols.

00:00:36.235 --> 00:00:39.235
on this structure and it's
attached to three carbons.

00:00:39.675 --> 00:00:43.065
I always recommend drawing it this
way and what this is going to connect

00:00:43.074 --> 00:00:46.815
to is to three separate fatty acids.

00:00:47.205 --> 00:00:51.744
So whenever we have a fatty acid
chain I replace the R group, uh, the

00:00:51.745 --> 00:00:53.754
long carbon chain with an R group.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:58.130
Usually what these look like
is these giant long chains

00:00:58.130 --> 00:00:59.339
just like the one up here.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:04.390
So we have the glycerol right here on the
far left and we can see like little tiny

00:01:04.399 --> 00:01:07.070
polar region of the fat or the oil here.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:12.119
And then that's going to be connected
to this very, very long fatty acid.

00:01:13.095 --> 00:01:16.675
So when the way this reaction works
is pretty easy what we're going

00:01:16.675 --> 00:01:21.164
to be doing is removing water to
connect the two fragments So what

00:01:21.164 --> 00:01:23.554
I always do is I line up the h2os.

00:01:23.565 --> 00:01:31.224
So if you see here we got h2o H2o
with my little androids h2o and what

00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:36.565
we're gonna be doing is removing that
water piece Okay, so if I copy and

00:01:36.565 --> 00:01:42.785
paste this down right here and I move
it down below We are just going to

00:01:42.795 --> 00:01:45.225
be copying and pasting that piece.

00:01:45.825 --> 00:01:48.835
So we're getting rid of the
water part, just like so, boop

00:01:48.885 --> 00:01:50.795
boop, and deleting all those.

00:01:52.495 --> 00:01:56.205
And now what we're going to do is
merge these two parts together.

00:01:56.705 --> 00:02:02.615
So when that happens, we now have our
fatty acids that are now connecting.

00:02:03.015 --> 00:02:08.235
To our, our structure, and
this is what we call a glycerol

00:02:08.305 --> 00:02:11.594
carboxylate, or our triacylglyceride.

00:02:12.115 --> 00:02:16.094
Now keep in mind here, the ending,
right, so before if we had a fatty

00:02:16.095 --> 00:02:18.605
acid, it would be that carboxylic acid.

00:02:18.995 --> 00:02:22.874
But being that this is referring to
that it's, it's the salt form that

00:02:22.875 --> 00:02:24.805
this turns into before it bonds.

00:02:25.225 --> 00:02:28.705
It's going to have that 8
ending when we name these, okay?

00:02:28.705 --> 00:02:31.395
So it's glycerol, and it's
going to be a carboxylate.

00:02:31.405 --> 00:02:34.345
So when you see a name, you're
looking for the 8 ending when it's

00:02:34.345 --> 00:02:36.395
referring to the large structure.

00:02:41.564 --> 00:02:45.124
You want to draw the condensed
structural formula for glycerol.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.160
Tripalumetoliate.

